welcome back everyone we are all trying  to stay cool during this heat wave but  how do the record-breaking temperatures  impact your health  if you have heart problems well joining  us with some insight here at noon  is interventional cardiologist dr  anthony dota good afternoon to you  doctor it's great to have you  hi alex thanks for having me thanks for  making time for us wanted to ask you  first you know people with heart disease  are at a higher risk  for heat stroke why is that yeah um  you're absolutely right we know that  patients with certain cardiac conditions  like congestive heart failure heart  artery disease  and certain valvular conditions seem to  suffer from heat stroke much more  frequently than people without those  conditions and  what we what we found is that patients  with those conditions have a more  difficult time  dissipating heat from their body because  they're unable to sort of shunt  blood flow appropriately to the skin to  get rid of some of that heat and  perspire  like normal people would be able to so  not only do they get this condition  more frequently but when we do see cases  in patients with underlying cardiac  conditions  they tend to have more severe  manifestations of the heat stroke as  well  i think we've all heard about heat  stroke how dangerous is it  um so yeah so heat stroke that requires  hospitalization  um is a very serious condition and um  there are some case series that report  mortality rates as high as 20 percent um  so it's important that if you recognize  that that's what's clinically going on  with the patient  to seek medical attention to to get  appropriate therapy for this  okay so with that in mind what should  you do if you have underlying heart  condition someone watching right now  and the temperature say is above 90 or  even 100.  yes so you really have to be cautious  when the temperatures outside exceed  what your normal body temperature is  which as  most of us know is around 98.6 degrees  so  um in las vegas we we typically tell our  cardiac patients to avoid being outside  during the  hottest parts of the day and during this  time of year that's really from about  noon to 6 pm where we see the hottest  temperatures and  if for any reason you're uh you know  unable to avoid going out and being  exposed to these extreme temperatures  you know wearing loose light-colored  clothing can help  um staying in the shade obviously just  some of these sort of  common sense things and you know staying  well hydrated as well so  drinking both before exposure during and  after um whether you're thirsty or not  can help  prevent one of these problems before it  happens absolutely  and so what are the signs of heat stroke  what should we look out for  so um you know obviously the increase in  body temperature which people feel as a  subjective fever is  is the biggest thing uh and what we  typically see is you know  lightheadedness  uh nausea muscle weakness  severe generalized fatigue uh and then  the actual heat stroke uh  entity people get neurologic dysfunction  so um  you're seeing people that are confused  or have slurred speech or or can even be  comatose so  that's where that sort of stroke portion  of the of the term comes from so those  are the things that  that are you know should be alarm  symptoms to seek medical attention  yeah really important things dr anthony  dota thank you so much for  all of your advice and for making time  for us we really appreciate it hey  thanks for having me it was my pleasure  stay safe  you too and thank you for all the work  you've been doing the past few months i  know it's been  it's been hard so thank you so much
 

